# PR #27 (@juliankang4): every decode gemv warp issues prefetch.global.L2 for its whole weight row before the walk. The same-lease
# A/B (docs/cards/l2pref-a6000.card, 14 rounds, Qwen3.6-35B-A3B Q4_K_M, depth 6, n = 96, ctx 256, A6000) measured the candidate at
# 0.9570 +- 0.0006 of the base: step p50 4.9185 -> 5.1377 ms (mean of the 14 rounds' p50), +219.2 us a step, +4.46 %. This profile
# says which kernels carry it. Both arms are one binary pair at the same load key; the only difference is the PR's two commits.
#
# Decomposition [derived]. The step is one serial chain on the card (the next token waits on this step's argmax; 520 graph
# nodes), so the wall change is the sum of the per-kernel changes plus the change of the gaps between kernels. The prefetch adds
# no host, PCIe, NVMe or host-DRAM work (resident load, majflt 0), and a prefetch hint adds no gap: gaps are predicted unchanged
# (|delta gap| <= 5 us a step). So  sum over kernels of delta = 219 us +- the tracer's scatter (the A/B's step runs a few us
# longer than the window wall by the host turnaround, the same in both arms).
# Where it lands: the L2-room term. A warp prefetches its whole row at once, then walks it, so the lines in flight are
# resident warps x row bytes: 84 SMs x 48 warps = 4032 warps at most (the trace's registers and block size per launch fix the
# real count; read there). From the file's header (hidden 2048, 40 layers of which 10 full attention, 8 of 256 experts, ff 512,
# vocab 248320) and the PR's diff, per step [derived]: gate_up (experts.rs gate_up_row, Q4_K, gate and up row = 2304 B a warp):
# 40 x 9.4 = 377 MB, in flight 9.3 MB; the head (head_argmax.rs, Q6_K row 1680 B): 417 MB, 6.8 MB; the GDN attn_qkv (lib.rs
# Q6_K 1-column gemv, row 1680 B, 8192 rows): 30 x 13.8 = 413 MB, 6.8 MB; the Q4_K projections (proj.rs, 1152 B a row): 4.6 MB,
# the down (q6k_sel, K = 512, 420 B a row): 275 MB, 1.7 MB, the router (256 rows, one prefetch per layer) stay under the A6000's
# 6 MB L2 and are predicted 0 +- 10 us. A line pushed out before its load is read from DRAM twice. Crude overflow model (an
# upper bound: a warp frees lines as it walks): evicted fraction (in flight - 6 MB) / in flight, gate_up 35 %, head and qkv 12 %;
# extra DRAM bytes 132, 50 and 50 MB, at ~700 GB/s +188, +71 and +71 us = 330 us against the A/B's 219. Scaled to 219 the three
# are 125, 47 and 47 us, so the top two sum 172..259 (the scaled and the unscaled model), and the top three carry the step's
# loss with the other kernels near 0: the band's lower edge is the A/B card's decide-in share (80 % of 219 = 175 us), the upper
# edge the unscaled model. Which of the head and qkv is second, and whether a third carries 10 % or more, is the open term.
# Resource timeline of one step (base 4.92 ms): card chain ~4.7 ms (~1.9 GB a step [derived] -> ~400 GB/s mean, DRAM demand at
# 768 GB/s = 2.5 ms, so DRAM is ~53 % of the step's cycles: the step is not purely a bandwidth wall, the gemv kernels at the
# roofline are the ones that pay for extra bytes); host CPU: graph launch + argmax readback, serial after the card, ~0.1-0.2 ms;
# PCIe: one token id in and one out, ~20 us, inside the host turnaround; host DRAM and NVMe: 0. The wall is card + host
# turnaround in series, never a max over resources; the lever sits wholly in the card term, so nothing hides it.
# Wall of the two runs [derived]: the load (21.2 GB warm in the page cache, ~10 s) + 1 prefill pass + 7 replays (~40 ms) + nsys
# finalize and export (~15 s) + the tables (~5 s) = about 1.0-2.0 min each under the lease, 3-5 min with the sync.
kind: profile
question: which kernels lose with PR #27's L2 row prefetch on the A6000, and by how many microseconds a decode step each?
term: sum over the two kernels with the largest |cand - base| of the kernel time a decode step, us, mean over replay windows 2-7
  of generate_qwen3moe --seed-depth 6 -n 8 --ctx 256 --mode graph --time on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B Q4_K_M, A6000, cand (main dc49ae45 + the
  PR's two commits) minus base (dc49ae45)
unit: us
predict: 175..260
condition: one lease per arm through tools/ref/nsys-gpu.sh's lease_take with this card, the A6000 the timing card (the witness
  `card` block names it and its power limit; no [other-busy] and no compute process of another owner before or after, else void);
  the base arm first, then the candidate, each from its own tree and remote dir (the sync source is the tree whose box.sh runs).
  The runner's window boundary is named, not searched for: embed_rows_q4k count must equal N = 8 in each trace (1 prefill pass +
  7 replays), or no table (rc 3) and the run is void; each run exits 0 and prints `time step 1 .. 7` rows; both loads print the
  same `load` line and the capture line `nodes=520` (the A/B's), else the arms differ in more than the PR. The tracer's flags are
  the runner's (-t cuda --cuda-graph-trace=node --cuda-event-trace=false --sample=none --cpuctxsw=none). The replay window wall
  of each arm sits within 10 % of the A/B's step (4.92 / 5.14 ms) less the host turnaround, else the tracer stretched it: read
  only ratios. Commands: BLOOMERY_MODEL=qwen3moe BLOOMERY_REF_MODEL=/models/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B/Qwen3.6-35B-A3B-Q4_K_M.gguf
  BLOOMERY_REMOTE='~/repo/bloomery-l2pref-base' BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/l2pref-nsys.card'
  <that tree>/tools/box.sh 'BLOOMERY_NSYS_DEPTHS=6 BLOOMERY_NSYS_N=8 BLOOMERY_NSYS_TOP=40 bash tools/ref/nsys-gpu.sh'; the profile
  name qwen3moe is the runner's seed-form switch (nsys-gpu.sh: FORM=seed for MODEL_NAME=qwen3moe), the file is Qwen3.6's through
  BLOOMERY_REF_MODEL, which generate_qwen3moe reads the architecture from; the same with the candidate tree
decide-in: the two kernels carry the loss (their delta is 175 us or more, 80 % of the A/B's): post the per-kernel table to the PR
  (27) and ask the contributor to bound the prefetch by the rows in flight against the card's L2 on those two kernels (prefetch a
  row's head only, or not at all when the grid's in-flight bytes pass the L2); no further profile
decide-below: under 175 us: the loss is spread over three or more kernels, not two over-large in-flight sets: read the top five
  against their bytes, launch counts and the trace's registers and block size (the in-flight lines), and ask the contributor for a per-kernel lever (the prefetch on the kernels that
  gain, off on the rest) before landing; a losing kernel whose in-flight lines sit under 6 MB means the L2-room model is not its cause,
  and the next term is the issue-slot cost of the hint
decide-above: over 260 us: the two lose more than the step, so other kernels gain: first compare each arm's window wall with the
  A/B's step (a stretch of more than 10 % is the tracer's: read ratios only); if they match, read the gainers, which are the
  kernels the prefetch should stay on, and propose the PR keep it there only
